{"hash":"c027a56419bbcade","voice":"en-IN-NeerjaExpressiveNeural","rate":"+4%","dur":107.623,"said":"Stop here, at the head of the passage. Ahead, through the narrow silver door, is Shri Kedarnath. Look: there is no carved linga there, only a broad, rough rock, rising from the floor like the hump of a great animal. This is what the Lord left behind when he sank into the earth, and it is worshipped as Sadashiva. In the early hours, pilgrims come close, touch the rock with their hands, bow their heads to it and anoint it with ghee, a practice tradition links to the Pandavas themselves. This is the Lord who stayed for two sages, who hid from five brothers and forgave them, and whose house stood through the flood. Fold your hands. Adi Shankaracharya, who by tradition left his body here, sings of this place in the Dvadasha Jyotirlinga Stotram: ‘Mahadriparshve cha tate ramantam, sampujyamanam satatam munindraih, surasurair yaksha mahoragadyaih, Kedaram isham Shivam ekam ide.’ ‘He who delights on the flank of the great mountain, on its high slopes, worshipped always by the greatest sages, by gods and demons, yakshas and great serpents: I praise that One, the Lord Kedara, Shiva.’ Kedarnath is the eleventh Jyotirlinga of the Stotram. Pilgrims may visit the twelve Jyotirlingas in any order their journey allows; by tradition, the yatra begins at Somnath, the first, and is held complete with darshan at Grishneshwar, the twelfth, near Ellora. Let the bells ring and the lamps be raised. Jai Baba Kedar!\n\nDid you know? In 2025 the doors closed for the winter on 23 October, Bhai Dooj, after a season of more than 17 lakh pilgrims.","segments":[{"key":"text","start":0,"end":1412},{"key":"didYouKnow","start":1428,"end":1539}],"words":[[0.098,0.421,0,4],[0.531,0.325,5,9],[1.048,0.144,11,13],[1.204,0.12,14,17],[1.336,0.192,18,22],[1.54,0.12,23,25],[1.673,0.096,26,29],[1.781,0.613,30,37],[3.281,0.445,39,44],[3.966,0.18,46,53],[4.159,0.096,54,57],[4.267,0.409,58,64],[4.688,0.421,65,71],[